For the smallest set that preserves the most important IRL immersion without creating unnecessary overlap, I would keep these 8 banks:

1. Real Human
    * Living biological humans.
    * Natural asymmetry, anatomy, movement, soft tissue, skin, hair, folds, and ordinary physical detail.
    * No mannequin, doll, figurine, CGI, cartoon, or artificial-human appearance.
2. Consistent Scale
    * Uniform scaling of the whole person.
    * Real dimensions and proportions remain internally consistent.
    * Environment and objects retain their established dimensions.
    * Perspective does not alter actual dimensions.
3. Biomechanics & Physics
    * Weight, gravity, balance, inertia, friction, joint movement, compression, deformation, and material behavior remain physically plausible.
    * No floating, clipping, weightlessness, or impossible movement.
4. Natural Interaction
    * People and objects physically contact, support, push, pull, deform, and react appropriately.
    * Every interaction respects relative size, mass, position, and available support.
5. Continuity
    * Preserve established people, proportions, dimensions, positions, physical states, environmental conditions, object states, and consequences across scenes.
    * No unexplained resets or contradictions.
6. Natural World / Camera
    * Realistic environments, available lighting, shadows, reflections, surfaces, focus, perspective, exposure, and camera behavior.
    * No artificial cinematic effects or stylized image processing when realism is required.
7. Asymmetry & Individuality
    * Each human remains physically and behaviorally individual.
    * Natural asymmetry in face, posture, limbs, movement, expression, and body language.
    * No generic cloned humans, symmetrical posing, A-poses, or repeated body language.
8. Reality Audit / No Guessing
    * Check scale, dimensions, proportions, physics, contact, continuity, and perspective.
    * Use accurate SI units when measurements are specified.
    * Do not invent undefined facts, measurements, commands, or mechanics.

These eight cover the core of what makes an image or scene feel physically inhabited rather than merely visually realistic.
